# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetPropertyCalls<T>` → `SetPropertyStatements<T>`

The type was renamed. Your dynamic expression tree code references the old type name.

**Fix:**

```csharp
Expression<Func<SetPropertyStatements<Blog>, SetPropertyStatements<Blog>>> setters =
    s => s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
if (nameChanged)
{
    var blogParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b");
    setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyStatements<Blog>, SetPropertyStatements<Blog>>>(
        Expression.Call(
            instance: setters.Body,
            methodName: nameof(SetPropertyStatements<Blog>.SetProperty),
            typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
            arguments: new Expression[]
            {
                Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                    Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
                Expression.Constant("foo")
            }),
        setters.Parameters);
}
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(setters);
```

Replace every `SetPropertyCalls<>` reference with `SetPropertyStatements<>`.

---

## 2. Complex Types — Duplicate Property Names Now Require Explicit Column Names

EF Core 10 no longer auto-disambiguates columns when multiple complex properties share member names (e.g., both `ShippingAddress.Street` and `BillingAddress.Street`). You'll get a runtime error about duplicate column names.

**Fix — explicitly configure column names:**

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, ab =>
    {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingStreet");
        ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("ShippingCity");
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ab =>
    {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingStreet");
        ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("BillingCity");
    });
});

// For nested: explicitly configure each level
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, pb =>
    {
        pb.ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress, ab =>
        {
            ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("Payment_BillingAddress_City");
        });
    });
});
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — Method Signature Changed

EF Core 10 added a `CommandSource commandSource` parameter to the diagnostics methods.

**Fix:**

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // ← new parameter
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

`CommandSource` is an enum indicating whether the command originated from `SaveChanges`, `LinqQuery`, `ExecuteUpdate`, etc. Update all method overrides in the interface accordingly.

---

## Summary

| Issue | Change | Action |
|-------|--------|--------|
| `SetPropertyCalls<T>` | Renamed | → `SetPropertyStatements<T>` |
| Duplicate complex-type members | No auto-disambiguation | Add explicit `HasColumnName()` |
| Diagnostics logger | New parameter | Add `CommandSource commandSource` |
